# Cloudflare Pages response headers.
#
# The backend sets its own CSP, but that only covers API responses. This origin
# is the one that renders the app, holds session key material, and decrypts
# student PII in memory — it needs its own headers.
#
# 'wasm-unsafe-eval' is required by argon2-browser, zxing-wasm, busytex and
# EmbedPDF's PDFium engine.
# blob: covers the compiler worker, the EmbedPDF render worker (worker-src),
# and pdf.js rendering. PDF previews (compiled Angabe/Lösung, scanned
# submissions) are rendered in-page by EmbedPDF (see lib/pdf/embedpdf.ts)
# instead of an <iframe>/<object> pointed at a blob: URL, so frame-src no
# longer needs blob: for that — kept at 'self' only, in case a same-origin
# frame is ever needed elsewhere. connect-src carries blob: explicitly
# (rather than relying on the CSP spec's implicit same-origin blob: match)
# because EmbedPDF fetches the decrypted scan/compiled PDF via a blob: URL
# passed as its `src` config.
# camera=(self) keeps QR scanning working; every other permission is denied.
#
# connect-src must allow connection to the backend server origins configured by
# users in server mode: local loopback backends (http://localhost:*, http://127.0.0.1:*)
# as permitted by backendStore, plus any HTTPS backend. IPv6 loopback (::1) is
# intentionally omitted — Chrome's CSP parser rejects the [::1]:* form, and
# real users always connect to localhost or 127.0.0.1 rather than ::1.
#
# THIS FILE IS A TEMPLATE. The `__INLINE_SCRIPT_HASHES__` token in script-src
# is replaced during `npm run build` by scripts/generate-csp-headers.mjs, which
# hashes the inline scripts that actually ended up in build/**/*.html.
#
# SvelteKit injects an inline hydration bootstrap into every prerendered page,
# and that script hard-codes the content-hashed entry chunk filenames
# (`import("/_app/immutable/entry/start.<hash>.js")`). Those names change with
# any bundle change — including ones we do not control, such as a patch-level
# Vite/Rollup bump or a different Node version in the Pages build image — so a
# hash pinned here by hand goes stale and the deployed app dies with
# "Executing inline script violates the following Content Security Policy
# directive" while local dev keeps working. Never hard-code the hash again;
# leave the placeholder in place and let the build fill it in.
#
# If the build step is skipped the placeholder survives, browsers ignore the
# unknown source expression, and inline script stays blocked — fail-closed.
/*
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'wasm-unsafe-eval' __INLINE_SCRIPT_HASHES__; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self' data: blob:; font-src 'self'; connect-src 'self' blob: https: http://localhost:* http://127.0.0.1:*; worker-src 'self' blob:; frame-src 'self'; object-src 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'; base-uri 'self'; form-action 'self'
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Referrer-Policy: no-referrer
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(self), microphone=(), geolocation=(), payment=(), usb=()
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin

# pdf.js ships its worker as .mjs; make the JS MIME type explicit so the
# module worker is accepted regardless of the host's extension mapping.
/_app/immutable/assets/*.mjs
  Content-Type: text/javascript

# EmbedPDF's PDFium engine (lib/pdf/embedpdf.ts) ends up hashed into this
# directory via a Vite `?url` import — same belt-and-suspenders MIME
# override as pdf.worker's .mjs above.
/_app/immutable/assets/*.wasm
  Content-Type: application/wasm

/core/busytex/*.wasm
  Content-Type: application/wasm

/core/busytex/*.wasm.bin
  Content-Type: application/wasm

/wasm/*.wasm
  Content-Type: application/wasm
